Nmap done: 1 IP address (1 host up) scanned in 42.11 seconds', 1536);
DELETE FROM lab_tasks WHERE lab_id = 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first';
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_nmap-what-first_t1', 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first', 1, 'Which finding would you chase first, and why it rather than the obvious ones?', 'Redis on 6379 responding without authentication. It is unauthenticated access to a data store, reachable now, with no exploit chain required, and on many deployments it leads straight to writing files as the service user. The obvious ones, old MySQL and an expired certificate, are findings but neither hands you access in the first ten minutes.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_nmap-what-first_t2', 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first', 2, 'Anonymous FTP is open with a backups directory. What do you do, and what do you not do?', 'Log in, list the directory, and record what is exposed and its size. Do not download the backups: on an engagement you are proving exposure, not exfiltrating client data, and pulling a database backup onto your machine creates a liability you did not need. Note the filenames and dates, screenshot the listing, and describe the impact.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_nmap-what-first_t3', 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first', 3, 'The certificate expired in March 2025 and is self-signed. How do you rate that, honestly?', 'Low on its own, and say so rather than padding the report. It is a staging host; self-signed is common there and the real risk is that it trains staff to click through warnings. The more interesting observation is what it implies: a certificate nobody renewed for eighteen months suggests the host is unowned, which is usually true of the rest of its patching too.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_nmap-what-first_t4', 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first', 4, 'Jenkins on 8080 returns 403 and Elasticsearch on 9200 is open. Which is the bigger problem and why?', 'Elasticsearch, because 403 on Jenkins means something is refusing you while an open 9200 typically means the cluster API answers without authentication, so you can read every index. Jenkins is still worth revisiting, since 403 from the root does not mean every endpoint is protected and Jenkins is a credential store with deploy access when it is not. But start where the data is already readable.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_nmap-what-first_t5', 'lab_pentest_nmap-what-first', 5, 'The client says this is only staging, so none of it matters. Respond.', 'Ask one question: does staging hold production data, and can staging reach production. Both are usually yes, and the answer to either turns this into a production finding. Staging is where the controls are relaxed and the data is real, which is exactly why it gets attacked. Put the answer in the report in their words rather than arguing in the room.');

INSERT OR REPLACE INTO labs (id, title, role, level, brief, artifact_kind, artifact_name, is_free, created_at) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 'Same token, someone else''s invoice', 'pentest', 'mid', 'Five request and response pairs from your proxy during an authenticated API test. You are signed in as account 8812 throughout and never change the token. Work out what you have, and what you would write.', 'log', 'proxy-history.http', 0, '2026-11-20T16:36:32.973Z');
INSERT OR REPLACE INTO lab_artifacts (lab_id, body, bytes) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', '### Request 1
GET /api/v2/invoices/4471 HTTP/1.1
Host: billing.northfield.co.in
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.PAYLOAD_USER_8812.SIG
Accept: application/json

### Response 1
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"id":4471,"account_id":8812,"customer":"Rohit Menon","amount":"18400.00",
 "currency":"INR","status":"paid","pdf":"/api/v2/invoices/4471/pdf"}

### Request 2
GET /api/v2/invoices/4472 HTTP/1.1
Host: billing.northfield.co.in
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.PAYLOAD_USER_8812.SIG
Accept: application/json

### Response 2
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"id":4472,"account_id":9043,"customer":"Sunita Rao","amount":"226500.00",
 "currency":"INR","status":"unpaid","pdf":"/api/v2/invoices/4472/pdf"}

### Request 3
GET /api/v2/accounts/9043 HTTP/1.1
Host: billing.northfield.co.in
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.PAYLOAD_USER_8812.SIG

### Response 3
HTTP/1.1 403 Forbidden
Content-Type: application/json

{"error":"account_scope_mismatch"}

### Request 4
PATCH /api/v2/accounts/8812 HTTP/1.1
Host: billing.northfield.co.in
Authorization: Bearer eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.PAYLOAD_USER_8812.SIG
Content-Type: application/json

{"display_name":"Rohit M","role":"admin"}

### Response 4
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"id":8812,"display_name":"Rohit M","role":"admin","updated_at":"2026-11-19T11:42:08Z"}

### Request 5
GET /api/v1/invoices/4472 HTTP/1.1
Host: billing.northfield.co.in
Accept: application/json

### Response 5
HTTP/1.1 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

{"id":4472,"account_id":9043,"customer":"Sunita Rao","amount":"226500.00","status":"unpaid"}', 1711);
DELETE FROM lab_tasks WHERE lab_id = 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment';
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment_t1', 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 1, 'Requests 1 and 2 are the finding. State it precisely enough for a developer to act on.', 'GET /api/v2/invoices/{id} returns any invoice to any authenticated caller. The same bearer token for account 8812 returns invoice 4471 belonging to account 8812 and invoice 4472 belonging to account 9043. The endpoint authenticates the caller but never checks that the invoice belongs to them. Broken object-level authorisation, and the fix is an ownership check on the record, not on the route.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment_t2', 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 2, 'Request 3 returns 403. Why does that make the finding stronger rather than weaker?', 'Because it proves the application knows how to do this. /api/v2/accounts/9043 correctly refuses with account_scope_mismatch, so the authorisation model exists and was simply not applied to the invoices endpoint. That removes the "we never designed for this" defence and turns it from an architecture discussion into a missed check on one route, which is both more embarrassing and much easier to fix.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment_t3', 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 3, 'Request 4 is a second, different vulnerability. Name it and say what it costs.', 'Mass assignment. The PATCH to the caller''s own account sends a role field, which a client should never be able to set, and the response confirms role is now admin. Self-service privilege escalation from a normal account. Rate it above the invoice read: one discloses data, this one makes the attacker an administrator.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment_t4', 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 4, 'Request 5 drops the Authorization header entirely and still returns data. What have you actually found?', 'An old API version left routable with no authentication at all. /api/v1/invoices/4472 returns the record unauthenticated, which means the v2 access control conversation is beside the point while v1 is reachable. Retired versions that were never actually retired are one of the most common ways a well-secured API leaks, and nobody is monitoring them because nobody remembers they exist.');
INSERT INTO lab_tasks (id, lab_id, position, prompt, model_answer) VALUES ('lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment_t5', 'lab_pentest_idor-and-mass-assignment', 5, 'How do you order these three in the report, and what do you say about remediation sequence?', 'Unauthenticated v1 first, because it needs no account at all. Mass assignment second, because it gives an attacker admin. Invoice object access third. On sequence, say that fixing v2 while v1 is still routable fixes nothing, so turning off v1 is the immediate action and the authorisation work follows. Giving a remediation order rather than a severity list is what makes a report usable.');

INSERT OR REPLACE INTO labs (id, title, role, level, brief, artifact_kind, artifact_name, is_free, created_at) VALUES ('lab_cloud_leaked-key-in-the-trail', 'The deploy key woke up in another country', 'cloud', 'mid', 'Control-plane audit events from one cloud account for a single day, pulled after the billing alert showed data transfer out of a region you do not use. The deploy pipeline is supposed to be the only thing using automation credentials here.', 'table', 'audit-trail-1119.csv', 0, '2026-11-20T16:36:32.973Z');
